title: A daemon that hangs up mid-response returns a truncated body as a whole one
updated: 2026-08-28T00:26:18Z
---

Found while implementing karr #60, and NOT introduced by it: the behaviour is
identical before and after that change. It is a pre-existing defect of the
buffered readers and wants its own decision, because fixing it adds a fatal
path where there is none today.

The readers end their loops on `last unless $n`, which is the end of the
stream -- and they then return what they have as though the response were
complete. Nothing checks the body against what the response said it would be.

MEASURED, over an AF_UNIX socketpair whose peer writes the bytes below and
closes immediately (no daemon, no timeout involved; _read_response driven
directly):

  Content-Length: 11 announced, 9 bytes sent   -> returned 'hello wor'
  chunk announces 11 (b), 5 bytes sent         -> returned 'hello'
  one complete chunk, no terminating 0-chunk   -> returned 'hello'
  chunk header cut in half ("1" then EOF)      -> returned 'hello'

All four are truncation the caller cannot see. It is the same class as karr
#59's: a short body satisfies every return shape this role promises -- ndjson
gives an ArrayRef, raw gives bytes, the default gives a decoded body -- and is
indistinguishable from a complete one. A half tarball that looks whole is the
worst of them.

The fix is a structural completeness check in the buffered readers, most
naturally a read_exact helper next to _read_bytes: an EOF before Content-Length
is truncation, and so is one inside chunk data, inside a chunk header, or
before the terminating zero chunk. A close-delimited body is the one shape
where an EOF *is* the end, and must stay that way.

Why it was not folded into #60. It replaces a claim t/read_timeout.t makes on
purpose. That file drives every read site twice -- once for a timeout, once for
the same short read at a real end of response -- and asserts that the second
one does NOT croak, naming the returned truncation ('a truncated body is
returned as though whole when the response really ended -- the exact silent
loss the errno check prevents'). Making truncation fatal rewrites the
non-timeout half of site_ok in five subtests, so it is a redesign of #59's
safety net rather than an addition to it, and it wants to be a change someone
can read on its own.

Needs a Changes entry when it lands, and a live check on both engines: a new
fatal path could fire on a legitimate early close.

Related: #59, #60.
